CREATE VIEW historyView AS
SELECT
    history._id AS id,
    animes._id AS animeId,
    episodes._id AS episodeId,
    animes.title,
    animes.thumbnail_url AS thumbnailUrl,
    animes.source,
    animes.favorite,
    animes.cover_last_modified,
    episodes.episode_number AS episodeNumber,
    history.last_seen AS seenAt,
    history.origin AS origin,
    max_last_seen.last_seen AS maxSeenAt,
    max_last_seen.episode_id AS maxSeenAtEpisodeId
FROM animes
JOIN episodes
ON animes._id = episodes.anime_id
JOIN history
ON episodes._id = history.episode_id
JOIN (
    SELECT episodes.anime_id,episodes._id AS episode_id, MAX(history.last_seen) AS last_seen
    FROM episodes JOIN history
    ON episodes._id = history.episode_id
    GROUP BY episodes.anime_id
) AS max_last_seen
ON episodes.anime_id = max_last_seen.anime_id;

-- AM (HISTORY_ORIGIN) -->
-- :playerOnly = 1 hides rows the browse feed wrote.
--
-- The entry's most recent episode is picked HERE rather than taken from the
-- view's maxSeenAtEpisodeId, because that one is computed across both origins:
-- an entry whose newest row is a feed view would match no row at all once the
-- filter removed it, and would vanish from the list instead of falling back to
-- the last episode actually watched.
-- AM (A_HISTORY_IS_READ_A_PAGE_AT_A_TIME) -->
-- Was `history:`, selecting the whole list. SQLDelight notifies on every write
-- to the tables a query reads, and watching an episode writes a history row
-- continuously, so each progress save re-ran this query over the entire table
-- and rebuilt the screen's whole list. At the 102k rows measured below that is
-- 132 ms and a full re-materialisation per save, in a loop for as long as
-- playback runs - the cost is the repetition, not any single run.
--
-- Same query, with LIMIT and OFFSET so QueryPagingSource can ask for one page.
-- The ROW_NUMBER pass and everything recorded about it below is unchanged; the
-- ORDER BY has to stay for the window pick to mean anything, and it is what
-- makes the offset stable between pages.
-- <-- AM (A_HISTORY_IS_READ_A_PAGE_AT_A_TIME)
historyPaged:
-- AM (A_HISTORY_PICKS_THE_LATEST_IN_ONE_PASS): one window-function pass instead
-- of a correlated subquery over historyView.
--
-- The entry's most recent episode is still picked here rather than taken from
-- the view's maxSeenAtEpisodeId, for the reason above: that column is computed
-- across both origins, so an entry whose newest row is a feed view would match
-- no row once :playerOnly removed it, and would vanish instead of falling back
-- to the last episode actually watched.
--
-- What changed is how it is picked. It used to re-enter historyView once per
-- candidate row - and that view carries a GROUP BY aggregate over the whole
-- history table - so the per-anime "latest" was recomputed for every row that
-- reached the filter. ROW_NUMBER partitions by entry and orders by the same
-- (last_seen, episode_id) the subquery did, so the pick is made once for every
-- entry in a single pass, and the view is not involved at all.
--
-- Measured against the same data, identical result sets (both :playerOnly
-- values, with and without a :query): 5.9 -> 4.3 ms at 3k history rows,
-- 62 -> 37 ms at 32k, 197 -> 132 ms at 102k.
--
-- The view itself is now referenced by nothing - getLatestHistory below reads
-- the base tables too - so its max_last_seen aggregate is never evaluated.
-- Left in place because an unreferenced view costs nothing until something
-- selects from it, while dropping it needs a migration.
--
-- Worth recording why it was not simply slimmed earlier: nothing reads
-- maxSeenAt or maxSeenAtEpisodeId, so the join looked like free weight, but
-- removing it while the view was still in the query path measured SLOWER (261
-- vs 197 ms at 102k) - the aggregate gave the planner a per-entry table it used
-- to drive the join. The columns were unused; the join was not.
WITH latest AS (
    SELECT
    EP.anime_id AS animeId,
    H._id AS id,
    H.episode_id AS episodeId,
    H.last_seen AS seenAt,
    EP.episode_number AS episodeNumber,
    -- AM (A_HISTORY_FILTERS_ON_WHAT_IT_KNOWS): carried for the downloaded
    -- filter, which cannot be expressed here - a download is a folder on disk,
    -- not a row - so it is applied in Kotlin against the download cache, and
    -- that cache is keyed by episode name, scanlator, entry title and source.
    EP.name AS episodeName,
    EP.scanlator,
    EP.url AS episodeUrl,
    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
        PARTITION BY EP.anime_id
        ORDER BY H.last_seen DESC, H.episode_id DESC
    ) AS rn
    FROM history H
    JOIN episodes EP
    ON EP._id = H.episode_id
    WHERE H.last_seen > 0
    AND (:playerOnly = 0 OR H.origin = 0)
    -- AM (A_A_GLANCE_IS_NOT_A_VIEWING) -->
    -- :minSecondsSeen drops rows whose episode was barely watched. 0 is off,
    -- which is also the default, so the predicate collapses to true and the
    -- query is the one it was.
    --
    -- Two escapes, and both are needed rather than being belt and braces.
    --
    -- EP.seen: a finished episode's stored position is ZERO, because finishing
    -- it resets last_second_seen (see updateEpisodeProgressOnComplete). A
    -- filter on position alone would therefore hide precisely the episodes
    -- watched all the way through, which is the opposite of what it is for.
    --
    -- The position test is a floor on a scroller card too, not a measure of it:
    -- a feed card loops, so a five-second clip watched for two minutes never
    -- reports a position above five seconds. Those are caught by EP.seen as
    -- well - a card that laps is marked seen (SCROLLER_MARKS_SEEN) - so what
    -- the position test is left deciding is the case it is good at: something
    -- opened, held briefly and abandoned, which is neither seen nor far in.
    --
    -- Applied in the CTE, before the ROW_NUMBER pick, for the same reason
    -- :playerOnly is: an entry whose newest row is a glance should fall back to
    -- the last episode genuinely watched rather than disappear from the list.
    AND (:minSecondsSeen = 0 OR EP.seen = 1 OR EP.last_second_seen >= :minSecondsSeen * 1000)
    -- <-- AM (A_A_GLANCE_IS_NOT_A_VIEWING)
    -- AM (A_HISTORY_FILTERS_ON_WHAT_IT_KNOWS) -->
    -- :watchedOnly = 1 keeps only episodes that reached the watched threshold.
    -- "Watched" and "viewed" are different states here: everything in this list
    -- is viewed by definition, and seen is the narrower one.
    --
    -- In the CTE with the others, so an entry whose newest row is unwatched
    -- falls back to its last watched episode rather than leaving the list.
    AND (:watchedOnly = 0 OR EP.seen = 1)
    -- <-- AM (A_HISTORY_FILTERS_ON_WHAT_IT_KNOWS)
)
SELECT
L.id,
L.animeId,
L.episodeId,
A.title,
A.thumbnail_url AS thumbnailUrl,
A.source,
A.favorite,
A.cover_last_modified,
L.episodeNumber,
L.seenAt,
-- AM (A_HISTORY_FILTERS_ON_WHAT_IT_KNOWS)
L.episodeName,
L.scanlator,
L.episodeUrl
FROM latest L
JOIN animes A
ON A._id = L.animeId
WHERE L.rn = 1
AND lower(A.title) LIKE ('%' || :query || '%')
-- AM (A_HISTORY_IS_READ_A_PAGE_AT_A_TIME): L.id breaks ties. seenAt alone is
-- not a total order - two entries watched in the same second compare equal, and
-- SQLite leaves the order of equal sort keys undefined. That did not matter when
-- one query returned the whole list; with OFFSET it means two separate
-- executions could order a tied pair differently and drop or repeat a row across
-- a page boundary. Not reproduced on this schema (the planner picks the same
-- plan for both pages, so the order is stable in practice), but stability by
-- coincidence of plan is not a guarantee. L.id is the history row id and the
-- rn = 1 pick leaves one per entry, so this makes the order total, and it only
-- orders pairs whose relative order was unspecified before.
ORDER BY L.seenAt DESC, L.id DESC
LIMIT :limit OFFSET :offset;

-- AM (A_HISTORY_IS_READ_A_PAGE_AT_A_TIME) -->
-- How many rows historyPaged would return, for the paging source's bounds.
--
-- Not the same query wrapped in COUNT(*). historyPaged picks one row per entry
-- with ROW_NUMBER, so its row count is just the number of entries that have a
-- qualifying history row and a matching title - which EXISTS answers without
-- the window function, without the join to episodes in the outer query, and
-- without the sort. Same filters, in the same order, so the two cannot drift on
-- what qualifies: last_seen > 0, the origin filter, and the title LIKE.
--
-- EXISTS rather than a join or IN (SELECT DISTINCT ...) for the reason recorded
-- on getSeenAnimeNotReachableFromLibraryAfter in animes.sq: it stops at the first
-- matching row per entry instead of building the whole set.
-- <-- AM (A_HISTORY_IS_READ_A_PAGE_AT_A_TIME)
historyCount:
SELECT COUNT(*)
FROM animes A
WHERE lower(A.title) LIKE ('%' || :query || '%')
AND EXISTS (
    SELECT 1
    FROM history H
    JOIN episodes EP
    ON EP._id = H.episode_id
    WHERE EP.anime_id = A._id
    AND H.last_seen > 0
    AND (:playerOnly = 0 OR H.origin = 0)
    -- AM (A_A_GLANCE_IS_NOT_A_VIEWING): the same predicate as historyPaged's,
    -- so the count cannot disagree with the list about what qualifies.
    AND (:minSecondsSeen = 0 OR EP.seen = 1 OR EP.last_second_seen >= :minSecondsSeen * 1000)
    -- AM (A_HISTORY_FILTERS_ON_WHAT_IT_KNOWS): the same predicate as
    -- historyPaged's. The downloaded filter is absent from both, so the count
    -- is the count before it - which is what enablePlaceholders = false makes
    -- harmless, and the reason that filter cannot live in this layer.
    AND (:watchedOnly = 0 OR EP.seen = 1)
);

-- <-- AM (HISTORY_ORIGIN)

-- AM (A_LATEST_HISTORY_READS_ONE_ROW): the newest row, read as one row.
--
-- It used to come through historyView, which carries a GROUP BY aggregate over
-- the whole history table (see max_last_seen above) - so answering "what was
-- watched last" grouped every history row in the database and then took one.
-- Reading the three tables directly lets idx_history_last_seen walk last_seen
-- descending and stop at the first match.
--
-- Same row set as the view: its max_last_seen join is one row per anime_id, so
-- it never multiplied or dropped a history row. Ties on last_seen were resolved
-- arbitrarily before and still are - there is no tiebreak either way.
--
-- This is what GetNextEpisodes and GetQueueResumePoint ask for, so it is on the
-- history screen's path rather than a background one.
getLatestHistory:
SELECT
H._id AS id,
EP.anime_id AS animeId,
H.episode_id AS episodeId,
A.title,
A.thumbnail_url AS thumbnailUrl,
A.source,
A.favorite,
A.cover_last_modified,
EP.episode_number AS episodeNumber,
H.last_seen AS seenAt,
-- AM (A_HISTORY_FILTERS_ON_WHAT_IT_KNOWS): the same three columns historyPaged
-- carries, in the same order, because both queries are read by the same mapper
-- - HistoryMapper.mapHistoryWithRelations - and a mapper is shaped by the
-- columns, so adding them to one query without the other stops this one
-- compiling. Unused here: nothing asks whether the newest row is downloaded.
EP.name AS episodeName,
EP.scanlator,
EP.url AS episodeUrl
FROM history H
JOIN episodes EP
ON EP._id = H.episode_id
JOIN animes A
ON A._id = EP.anime_id
WHERE H.last_seen > 0
ORDER BY H.last_seen DESC
LIMIT 1;
